State the accent colour instead of generating it.

aerium-brand-surfaces.patch, which this sits on top of, says in as many words
that it sets surfaces only and leaves the accent to the generated palette: that
kColorSysPrimary already resolves to a blue from our seed, and that overriding
text and accent tokens as well would be re-deriving readability by hand for no
gain. That was right when nothing else in the browser stated an accent. It is
no longer, and this reverses that one line of it deliberately.

What changed is that three other places now state the accent outright. The new
tab page draws #7FC4E4 in dark and #2C6BAE in light; aerium-webui-style.patch
gives the same two values to every WebUI page; aerium-settings-style.patch uses
them for links, toggles, buttons and the section labels. Leaving the native
toolbar, menus and dialogs on whatever Material 3 derives from the #2C6BAE seed
guarantees two accents in one window - and a generated tone is not the seed, it
is a tone-40 or tone-80 step away from it, so they were never going to agree by
accident.

DELIBERATELY TWO TOKENS. kColorSysPrimary and kColorSysOnPrimary, in the mixer
that patch already adds, and nothing else. The readability argument it makes
still holds for every on-surface text token, so those stay generated; what is
overridden is one accent and the colour drawn on top of it. Both are stated per
scheme rather than derived, which is the whole point.

The two values are the Material roles they replace, not arbitrary. In dark mode
primary is a light tone and on-primary a dark one, so #7FC4E4 sits on #06283D -
the same pair the new tab page uses for its accent chips. In light mode primary
is a mid tone on white, so #2C6BAE on white, which is the seed itself and the
core of the lens in the logo.

The IDs were read out of ui/color/color_id.h at 152.0.7977.82 rather than
recalled: kColorSysPrimary and kColorSysOnPrimary are declared on consecutive
lines, in the same block as kColorSysBaseContainerElevated, which the surfaces
patch already assigns.

Verified against the real 152.0.7977.82 source. The pristine file was fetched at
that tag, aerium-brand-surfaces.patch applied to it, this patch generated from
the result, and then re-applied to a clean copy of that same baseline: it lands
with no fuzz and reproduces the file byte for byte. Nothing else in the series
touches chrome_color_mixer.cc, checked by grepping patches/ for the filename.

--- a/chrome/browser/ui/color/chrome_color_mixer.cc
+++ b/chrome/browser/ui/color/chrome_color_mixer.cc
@@ -1191,6 +1191,8 @@
         SkColorSetRGB(0x14, 0x1F, 0x44)};
     aerium_mixer[ui::kColorSysBaseContainerElevated] = {
         SkColorSetRGB(0x1B, 0x2A, 0x57)};
+    aerium_mixer[ui::kColorSysPrimary] = {SkColorSetRGB(0x7F, 0xC4, 0xE4)};
+    aerium_mixer[ui::kColorSysOnPrimary] = {SkColorSetRGB(0x06, 0x28, 0x3D)};
   } else {
     aerium_mixer[ui::kColorSysBase] = {SK_ColorWHITE};
     aerium_mixer[ui::kColorSysHeader] = {SkColorSetRGB(0xD8, 0xE6, 0xF5)};
@@ -1200,5 +1202,7 @@
     aerium_mixer[ui::kColorSysBaseContainer] = {
         SkColorSetRGB(0xE9, 0xF1, 0xFB)};
     aerium_mixer[ui::kColorSysBaseContainerElevated] = {SK_ColorWHITE};
+    aerium_mixer[ui::kColorSysPrimary] = {SkColorSetRGB(0x2C, 0x6B, 0xAE)};
+    aerium_mixer[ui::kColorSysOnPrimary] = {SK_ColorWHITE};
   }
